\section{Notes}{

This is the main object provided by this package.

Contains information about the CNV segments
the purity and ploidy, and effective ploidy of the samples,
and metadata about the method name, sample name and the
reference genome used

The object contains segments which are produced by the CNV calling method and potentially
transformed using the functions within this package.

The segment information of the object is ammended with the following  columns:

\code{total.allele.copies}: integer of total allele copies for a segment
\code{major.allele.copies}: integer of major allele copies for a segment
\code{minor.allele.copies}: integer of minor allele copies for a segment
\code{cnv.annotation}     : A controlled list of annotation terms (e.g. NEUT, AMP, DEL). See \link{annotateCNV} for more information
\code{cnv.lrr}            : logR of this segment (0 == NEUTRAL)
\code{cnv.markers}        : Humber of heterozygous markers used in this segment
\code{cnv.totalmarkers}   : Number of markers (SNPs) included in this segment
\code{cnv.cf}            : Cancer cell fraction carrying this CNV. If not provided it is set to the purity of the sample
\code{cnv.loh}            : Is this a segment with Loss Of Heterozygosity ?
\code{cnv.markers_density}: Average density of cnv.markers (cnv.markers/width of segment)
\code{cnv.totalmarkers_density} : Average density of total markers
\code{cnv.fill}           : Was this segment included in the original prediction or added by the fillGaps function?

When a new object is created the segments should contain the columns listed above
The cnv.annotation is added by the function  \code{cnvstrmliner::annotateCNV}
The effective ploidy is added by the function \code{cnvstrmliner::calculateEffectivPloidy}
cnv.loh is calculated automatically
